# The Borrow Contract Ledger of Nikaia's standard library.
#
# Part III 13.5 says a published package ships its ledger, so that a consumer
# builds against contracts rather than guesses. This is that file for `std`, and
# it is the one a program's compiler reads when the program calls `io::…` or
# `fs::…` - what a caller has to know about a function it cannot see the body
# of: whether it may pause (`sync`), whether it may fail and with what
# (`throws`), and what its result may point into (`returns`).
#
# **Every entry here names what it throws** (ADR-280 D5). That was not always
# so: every one of them wrote `throws = ["?"]` - *something this compiler
# cannot name*, ADR-024 D1's absence of a claim - because these functions fail
# with Rust's errors and no Nikaia type stood for one. Nine entries throw, and
# the measurement was that seven of them fail with **one** type; it is
# `io::IoError` now, and `Overtaken` answers the other two. A caller that only
# passes a failure on reads the column as "it fails", which is what the boolean
# before it said; one that takes it apart has a name to match on.
#
# **Two origins, and the difference is not hidden.** The entries for modules
# written in Nikaia are *inferred* by the Stage 0 compiler from their source,
# and `crates/nikaia/tests/contracts.rs` regenerates them and fails if this file
# has drifted. The entries for the modules that are still Rust cannot be
# inferred by a compiler that does not read Rust, so they are **written here and
# reviewed like code**. That is the honest shape of a library whose
# implementation is half in another language, and the share that is written by
# hand shrinks as more of `std` becomes Nikaia (ADR-014).
#
# An absent `sync` means *not* sync - which is the promise this file exists to
# make. An absent *entry* means nothing is known, and a caller may not assume.
#
# **`keeps` reads the same way** (ADR-094 D2): absent on a *present* entry says
# the function keeps none of what it is given, so a caller may lend them. The
# six entries below that carry one are the ones whose argument outlives the call
# - `Vec::push`, `String::push`, `HashMap::entry`, `Entry::or_insert` and the
# two `set` doors. Every other `std` function reads what it is handed, which is
# what makes the column worth having: a caller that had to assume the worst
# about `str::len` would have to own everything it ever passes.
#
# **Present, `sync` says which kind of promise it is** (ADR-288). `sync = true`
# is one the source made and the compiler checked; `sync = "inferred"` is one
# the body implies, derived from the calls it makes. A caller uses them
# identically - both mean "this cannot pause" - and a *diff* must not, because
# withdrawing a promise someone wrote is a decision and losing an inferred one
# is a consequence of an edit somewhere else. Every entry below that is written
# by hand is `true` by definition: a promise about a Rust body, made here.
#
# **A parameter whose Rust type is a bound rather than a type is recorded `?`.**
# `fs::write(path: impl AsRef<Path>, data: impl AsRef<[u8]>)` accepts a `String`
# and a `ref String` and a buffer, so writing `ref String` here would make the type checker
# reject `fs::write(path, text)` for a `String` - a correct program, refused.
# `?` is the absence of a claim (ADR-024 D1) and it is the true one: the arity
# is still checked, and the type is not claimed because there is no one type.
#
# `touches` says which resources a function reaches and whether it changes them
# (ADR-292, Part I 8.1.1). It is what lets two operations that meet on nothing
# run at the same time. **Absent means it touches everything**, which is the
# opposite of how an absent `throws` reads and is deliberate: an effect nobody
# described has to order against everything, or a missing contract would make a
# program wrong rather than merely slow. Only the entries below say it; every
# other function in this file keeps the order it was written in.
#
# The resources it may name are a **closed list** - `file(path)`, `stdin`,
# `stdout`, `stderr`, `args` - kept in `contracts::touch`. It is closed because
# two touches of different kinds never conflict, so a kind the compiler did not
# know would be disjoint from every kind there is: a typo here would buy an
# overlap rather than fail to buy one. A word from outside the list is answered
# the way ADR-292 D3 answers everything it cannot read - the function reaches
# everything and keeps its place - and the list grows when a function needs it
# (ADR-288 D11), never before.
#
# **`touches = []` is a claim about resources and not about memory.** A function
# that changes something it was handed - `Vec::push`, `String::push_str`,
# `HashMap::entry` - reaches no *resource*, and writing `touches = []` on it
# would still be true in ADR-292 D3's terms and wrong to write: the ordering
# analysis finds a data dependency by comparing what a statement binds against
# what the next one mentions, and `xs.push(a)` twice binds nothing and mentions
# `xs` in both. Memory is the borrow checker's question (ADR-292 D3) and this
# analysis does not have its answer, so the functions that change what they are
# given say nothing here and keep their order. The same goes for a function
# that runs a lambda - `Vec::sort_by_key`, `Entry::and_modify` - where a
# `touches = []` would be a claim about somebody else's code.
#
# **And `sync`'s answer to that does not transfer here.** `sync = "sync(f)"`
# (ADR-288 D15) lets a higher-order function say "the lambda decides", sound
# because `sync` is a fact about a whole *function* and the lambda's body is
# walked as part of the function that writes it. `touches` is consumed per
# **statement**, and nothing walks a lambda for an effect - so "the lambda
# decides", read as "this call adds nothing", would buy an overlap on an
# incomplete touch set. There is no `touches = "sync(f)"`, and the entry a
# higher-order function would need says *add* what the lambda reaches rather
# than assume it away (ADR-288 D15, and `contracts::order`'s `Expr::Closure`
# arm).
#
# `crosses` on a `[type."…"]` says a value of that type **may cross a thread**
# (ADR-005 §1 Group B, `NK25xx`). It is only ever written for a type whose parts
# are Rust: a Nikaia `struct` records its `fields` and the check walks those,
# structurally, which is what that record asks for. Its absence is "nobody said"
# and not "it may not" - and "nobody said" is not permission, so the compiler
# will not put such a value on a thread of its own choosing.
#
# `sharing` on a `[fn."…"]` says which of a function's `Shared` positions are one
# allocation and which reference count each of those classes gets (ADR-037 D7).
# No `std` entry has one. `Shared::deref` is the only entry that mentions the
# type, and it takes the handle as a **borrow** and hands back a view of what is
# inside - so it has no `Shared` position of its own to classify: a borrow
# duplicates nothing (ADR-312 D1). `a_std_entry_that_takes_a_shared_needs_a_second_look`
# in `crates/nikaia/tests/sharing.rs` is what makes somebody look on the day an
# entry takes a handle by value or hands one back: the analysis treats a described
# callee as accounted for, so an entry that could keep a handle and cross a thread
# with it is a decision and not a line.
#
# `provenance` marks a **source**: a function whose result is bytes that entered
# the program from outside, and who chose them (ADR-010 D2). Its absence is not
# "trusted" - it is "this is not a source", which is what almost every function
# is. Every source `std` has today is the operator's own: a file they named, a
# pipe they connected, the arguments they typed. The untrusted ones - `http`,
# `net`, `db` - arrive with the modules that have them.
version = 3

# --- Written by hand: the modules that are still Rust ------------------------

# The arguments the program was started with. Read-only and read by anyone:
# two `cli::args()` calls hand back the same vector, so they meet on nothing
# and neither has to wait for the other. It is the most-refused callee in
# `examples/` - ten adjacent pairs named it - and it was refused for no better
# reason than that nobody had written this line.
# The arguments the program was started with.
#
# The program's own name is at 0, so the first one a user typed is at 1. Two
# calls meet on nothing and neither waits for the other.
[fn."cli::args"]
pub = true
# `sync` because there is nothing here to pause on: the arguments are a vector
# the process was started with, and reading them is memory. It had no `sync`
# line for want of somebody classifying it rather than because anybody decided
# it could pause - and an absent `sync` means *not* sync, which is what this
# file's own note above says. Found by ADR-055 §6 step 3: with `std`'s pausing
# entries awaited, `cli::args()` became `cli::args().await` and `Args` is not a
# future.
sync = true
touches = ["args read"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "() -> Args"

# The argument at a position, and **there may not be one** - which is the half
# the corpus writes every time: `cli::args().nth(1) ?? "default"` opens six of
# the example programs. Without this entry `nth` was a method nothing described,
# so the whole `??` stood on a value of no type; with it, the `String?` is what
# the coalescing operator is for (Part I 2.3, 3.5).
#
# `sync` and `touches` are `cli::args`' own, for its reason: reading the vector
# the process was started with is memory.
# The argument at a position, or nothing where there is none.
#
# There may not be one, which is why this hands back a `String?`: the shape the
# examples write is `cli::args().nth(1) ?? some_default`.
[fn."Args::nth"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["args read"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "(ref mut self, at: i64) -> String?"

# Write text to standard error, with no line break after it.
[fn."eprint"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["stderr write"]
locks = true
signature = "(text: ?)"

# Write text to standard error, and end the line.
[fn."eprintln"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["stderr write"]
locks = true
signature = "(text: ?)"

# **A file written through a buffer** ([ADR-297](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-297.md)
# D5): the first type here whose cleanup needs I/O. `write` holds, `flush`
# writes, and a writer nobody flushed is flushed by its cleanup where it dies -
# which can fail, so the function that owns one can fail with it.
# A file to write, empty: made where it is not there, emptied where it is.
#
# What is written to it is held until it is flushed, closed, or done with; being
# done with it flushes it, and that can fail, so a function that owns one can
# fail with it.
[fn."fs::create"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) write"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
signature = "(path: ref fs::Path, root: ref fs::Root) -> Writer"

# A file being written, through a buffer.
[type."fs::Writer"]
pub = true
crosses = true

[impl."Cleanup for fs::Writer"]

# Add text to what the file will hold. No I/O: it is written when the writer
# is flushed, closed or done with.
[fn."fs::Writer::write"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(mut self: ref Writer, text: ?)"

# Write what is held to the file now.
[fn."fs::Writer::flush"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) write"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
signature = "(mut self: ref Writer)"

# What being done with a writer does: it is flushed.
[fn."fs::Writer::cleanup"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) write"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
signature = "(mut self: ref Writer)"

# **Where a value with a cleanup dies** ([ADR-297](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-297.md)
# D2). The compiler records one of these as a call wherever such a value may
# die, and the analyses read it as they read any call: it pauses, and - where
# the value's cleanup can fail - it fails with a `cleanup::Failure`, which names
# each value and its message. It travels in the box and has no `type` entry of
# its own: the settle point is where it joins a failure already on its way, and
# only the box takes one of each. No program writes either.
[fn."cleanup::settle"]
touches = []
signature = "()"

[fn."cleanup::settle_failing"]
touches = []
throws = ["cleanup::Failure"]
signature = "()"

# **`close()`**, on any value with a cleanup (ADR-297 D4): the cleanup now, its
# failure this call's, and nothing owed afterwards.
# Run the value's cleanup now, and hand back its failure as this call's.
#
# Nothing is owed afterwards: the value is gone.
[fn."cleanup::close"]
pub = true
touches = []
throws = ["cleanup::Failure"]
signature = "(self: ?)"

# A file as memory, without reading it into any of its own.
#
# What comes back reads as text and the pages arrive as they are touched, which
# is what makes a file larger than memory workable. It fails where the file
# cannot be opened.
[fn."fs::map"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) read"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "(path: ref fs::Path, root: ref fs::Root) -> Mapped"

# The mapped file, as text.
#
# It is a **view** of the mapping and not a copy, so it lives as long as the
# mapping does and costs nothing to take.
[fn."fs::Mapped::deref"]
pub = true
sync = true
# A mapping is memory that *is* a file, so reading through it reads the file -
# and which file is the mapping's rather than a parameter this line could name.
# An unnamed read conflicts with every file touch, which is the safe direction:
# `[]` would claim an `fs::write` to the same file may be reordered across it.
touches = ["file read"]
signature = "(self: ref Mapped) -> ref(self) String"

# The whole of a file, as bytes.
#
# It hands back one shared buffer and not a copy, so passing the file on costs a
# reference count. It fails where the file cannot be read. The bytes came from
# the program's own machine, so what it hands back is **trusted**.
[fn."fs::read"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) read"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "(path: ref fs::Path, root: ref fs::Root) -> Bytes"

# The whole of a file, as text.
#
# It fails where the file cannot be read or is not UTF-8. The bytes came from
# the program's own machine, so what it hands back is **trusted**: a map keyed
# on it is hashed with the fast function.
[fn."fs::read_to_string"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) read"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "(path: ref fs::Path, root: ref fs::Root) -> String"

# Every file under a directory, as names relative to it, in sorted order.
# Directories are not listed, and each is visited once.
#
# Under `fs::Root::Dir` a name the walk finds that leads out of the root - a
# symlink pointing away - is left out rather than failing the walk. It fails
# where the directory itself cannot be read; a name that is not text is listed
# as the platform's bytes (ADR-319 D7). The names came from the program's own machine, so they are **trusted**
# (ADR-290 D14).
[fn."fs::walk"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) read"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "(path: ref fs::Path, root: ref fs::Root) -> Vec[fs::Path]"

# Whether a file or directory is there (Part III 17.1). The root comes first
# (ADR-108 D1): a name that leaves an `fs::Root::Dir` throws `Outside` rather
# than answering `false`. `sync`: one `stat`, as `fs::scratch` is one call.
[fn."fs::exists"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["file(path) read"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
signature = "(path: ref fs::Path, root: ref fs::Root) -> bool"

# **A test's own directory, as a root** (ADR-247 D5). `sync`: making one
# directory is one system call, the same size of blocking as `cli::args`'s read.
# A fresh, empty directory, as an `fs::Root::Dir`.
#
# What a test hands a function that writes, so that what it writes stays inside a
# place of the test's own. Under `nikaia test` it is inside the test's own
# directory, removed when the test ends; elsewhere it is a new directory under the
# system's temporary directory. Every call is a new one.
[fn."fs::scratch"]
pub = true
sync = true
throws = ["io::IoError"]
touches = ["file write"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "() -> fs::Root"

# Write data to a file.
#
# `create` makes the file where there is none and `append` adds to the end
# rather than replacing what is there. It fails where the path cannot be
# written.
[fn."fs::write"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) write"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
signature = "(path: ref fs::Path, root: ref fs::Root, data: ?; append: bool = false, create: bool = true)"

# Claim that `cond` holds whenever this line is reached.
#
# A false claim stops the program, as `panic` does, and says the claim as it
# was written and the value of each operand of its comparison. The condition
# may not pause, fail, touch anything or change a value, so that the compiler
# can later prove it and leave the check out (ADR-269).
[fn."assert"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(cond: bool; message: ref String = \"\")"

# Write text to standard output, with no line break after it.
[fn."print"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["stdout write"]
locks = true
signature = "(text: ?)"

# Write text to standard output, and end the line.
[fn."println"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["stdout write"]
locks = true
signature = "(text: ?)"

# The same text, with the five characters HTML reads as markup written as
# entities.
#
# What comes back **borrows** its argument where nothing had to change, so text
# with no markup in it costs no allocation.
[fn."html::escape"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
returns = "ref(text)"
signature = "(text: ref String) -> ?"

# Promise that `markup` is already HTML: the one constructor of `html::Raw`,
# and the one line to review, because everything downstream of it trusts this
# call.
[fn."html::Raw::new"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(markup: ?) -> ?"

# The whole of standard input, as bytes.
#
# It may pause rather than holding the thread it is called on, and it fails
# where the input cannot be read.
[fn."io::read"]
pub = true
throws = ["io::IoError"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "() -> Vec[u8]"

# **A produced sequence, and its step can fail**
# ([ADR-105](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-105.md) D1). It said `-> Lines`,
# a named type whose `iterates` column below carried the failing step - which
# worked for the `for` and for nothing else: `for line in io::lines()` bound a
# `line` of no type at all, so `line.len()` was a method on `?` and the six
# `.len()` calls of `examples/tally/src/main.nika` were among the 35 §1 counts.
#
# `Seq[String] pauses throws` says three things in one place: the element is a
# `String`, so the binding has a type; `throws` is the one spelling of *a step
# can fail*; and `pauses` is
# [ADR-172](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-172.md) D1's word for *a step
# suspends*, which is what makes a `for` over this give its thread up instead of
# holding it. It is a **positive** claim and not the absence of `sync`: the
# absence is *nobody said*, and a `map`'s step is exactly that.
# Standard input, one line at a time.
#
# The lines are produced as they are asked for rather than read up front, so a
# stream larger than memory is a `for` over this. A **step** can fail, because
# a step of it reads the pipe.
[fn."io::lines"]
pub = true
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "() -> Seq[String] pauses throws"

# The whole of standard input, as text.
#
# It pauses rather than holding the thread it is called on. It fails where the
# input is not UTF-8.
[fn."io::read_to_string"]
pub = true
throws = ["io::IoError"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "() -> String"

# Each element through a function, as a new list.
#
# Whether this pauses is the lambda's answer and not its own.
[fn."list::ListExt::map"]
pub = true
sync = "sync(f)"
# The list goes in by value and a new one comes out, which is what the language
# below's trait does; `U` is what the lambda comes to (ADR-293 D20).
signature = "[T, U](self: Vec[T], f: fn(T) -> U) -> Vec[U]"

# --- Written by hand: the collections and text the language below provides ---
#
# `Vec`, `HashMap`, `str` and `String` are Rust's, reached through Nikaia's
# spellings, and a compiler that does not read Rust cannot infer them. They are
# here because ADR-288 gave them a second job: the type checker resolves
# `xs.push(x)` only if it knows what `xs` is, and the `sync` inference (ADR-288)
# can only vouch for a method call the checker resolved. Every one of these was
# **observed being asked for** by a program in this repository; none is here
# speculatively, because a wrong entry does not merely fail to help - it makes
# the checker reject a correct program.
#
# Types are `?` wherever the honest answer is "no one type" (ADR-024 D1). What
# an entry does claim is its **arity**, which is checked - so the risk of being
# wrong here is real and the corpus is what holds it down.
#
# **A signature may name its receiver's type arguments** (ADR-288): `V` in
# `HashMap::entry` is whatever the map holds, bound at the call site and
# substituted away. A variable that stays unbound becomes `?`, never a name.
# The rule for where one may appear is narrow and deliberate: **in what flows
# out** - a result, a lambda's parameter - and never in an argument. What flows
# out is a promise this file makes and is wrong on its own account; what flows
# in is a constraint on somebody's program, and Rust's collections take more
# than their type parameters suggest (`get` by anything the key borrows as), so
# a variable there would reject correct code.
#
# **A higher-order method says `sync = "sync(f)"`.** Whether `and_modify` pauses
# depends on the lambda it is given, and the ledger can say "it depends" since
# ADR-288 D15: `sync(f)` names the parameter that decides, and a caller reads it
# as "this call adds no pausing of its own". `sync = true` here would be a
# promise about somebody else's code, and before `from` existed the honest
# alternative was to say nothing and cost every caller its inferred `sync`.
# The bound is ADR-288 D16: `from` is for a lambda that runs *before the call
# returns*, because that is the only case whose calls the caller has already
# counted. `a_detached_lambda_may_not_use_from` holds every `from` entry in this
# file to it.
#
# It does not generalise to the other columns. `throws` needs nothing - a
# lambda's failures are already in the enclosing function's error set, by the
# same walk - and `touches` must not be read this way at all, for the reason the
# header's note on `touches = []` gives.

# The number without its sign.
[fn."f64::abs"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> f64"

# The square root. A negative number gives not-a-number, which is what the
# hardware hands back rather than a failure.
[fn."f64::sqrt"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> f64"

# ## Arithmetic that does not abort (ADR-285 D6, D7)
#
# An overflow aborts, always (D1). These are how a program says it means
# something else, and they are named rather than spelled with a sign: both are
# rare, both are meant to be visible in the line that does them, and a sign for
# one would have to be written with `&`, which is borrowing here.
#
# **For `i32` and `i64` and no other integer type.** Part I 2.2 names those two,
# and ADR-288 D11's rule is that an entry exists because a program asked for it:
# the compiler accepts `u32` and the rest, but the specification does not offer
# them, so entries for them would be speculation about a surface that is not
# promised. The day Part I 2.2 grows, these grow with it.
#
# **No `saturating_shl` or `saturating_shr`**, because Rust has neither - D3 says
# "the same with `saturating_` wherever Rust has them" and this is where that
# clause bites.
#
# `touches = []` on each: a pure computation reaches no resource and binds its
# result, so two of them in a row may be reordered, which is what the header's
# note above asks the claim to mean.

# Addition that **wraps** at the ends of an `i32` instead of aborting.
[fn."i32::wrapping_add"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self, i32) -> i32"

# Subtraction that **wraps** at the ends of an `i32` instead of aborting.
[fn."i32::wrapping_sub"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self, i32) -> i32"

# Multiplication that **wraps** at the ends of an `i32` instead of aborting.
[fn."i32::wrapping_mul"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self, i32) -> i32"

# Division that **wraps** instead of aborting.
#
# The one case it changes is the most negative `i32` divided by `-1`, whose
# answer does not fit; a divisor of zero still aborts, because no result would
# be the right one.
[fn."i32::wrapping_div"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self, i32) -> i32"

# Negation that **wraps** instead of aborting: the most negative `i32`
# negates to itself.
[fn."i32::wrapping_neg"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> i32"

# The number without its sign, **wrapping** instead of aborting: the most
# negative `i32` comes back unchanged.
[fn."i32::wrapping_abs"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> i32"

# Shift left, with the number of places taken around the width of an `i32`
# rather than aborting. Bits shifted off the top are lost, as a shift always
# loses them.
[fn."i32::wrapping_shl"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self, places: ?) -> i32"

# Shift right, with the number of places taken around the width of an `i32`
# rather than aborting. The sign bit is carried in, because an `i32` is signed.
[fn."i32::wrapping_shr"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self, places: ?) -> i32"

# Addition that **stops** at the ends of an `i32` instead of aborting.
