# `Shared[T]` is the type several parts of a program own at once (Part I 6.2).
# It is here rather than inferred for the reason the whole of this section is
# here: what it expands to is Rust - a reference count chosen per value
# (ADR-037 D7), the atomic one where the value may reach another thread and the
# plain one where `contracts::sharing` proves it cannot. At
# `user_parallelism = no` every one of them is plain, because nothing can cross
# there (ADR-312 D10).
#
# **There is no constructor entry, and there must not be one.** The annotation
# *is* the constructor: `let db: Shared[Connection] = connect(…)` makes the
# first handle, and a `Shared::new` would write the same thing twice
# (ADR-312, Part I 6.2). There is no entry for making a second handle either,
# because a handle handed on by value is duplicated and there is nothing to call
# (ADR-312 D1).
#
# `deref` is the one entry, and it is what makes `serve(ref db)` mean something.
# ADR-042 D2 sees a container through to what it holds wherever its ledger
# records a `deref`, and D2's own note says `Shared` is seen through "the moment
# it has a `deref` entry, with no further decision". This is that entry. `T` is
# bound by the receiver (ADR-288), which is why one line answers for every
# `Shared` a program writes rather than for one instantiation.
# What the handle holds.
#
# It is what makes a `ref Shared[Connection]` reach a parameter declared
# `ref Connection`: a caller is not meant to notice the handle.
[fn."Shared::deref"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "[T](self: ref Shared[T]) -> ref(self) T"

