# Part II 12.2's four doors ([ADR-281](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-281.md)).
# Written down so the checker can resolve them: without an entry a call is "a
# method nothing describes", which costs the value its plain shape - and 12.2's
# own idiom cannot be `sync`, which `docs/mutex-floor.md` §3.1 found before these
# existed.
#
# `sync = "sync(f)"` (ADR-288 D15) on the two that take a lambda: the lambda
# decides, which is the whole of ADR-005 D6's rule - the door itself never
# pauses, and what runs inside it is the caller's own code. Waiting to acquire is
# not pausing: the rule is about a suspension point while a lock is *held*, and
# the panic hook must be `sync` and may block (Part I 7.2).
#
# `get` and `set` carry no `sync` line and need none, for a stronger reason than
# the other two have: while the lock is open in either, no code of the user's
# runs at all (ADR-281 D10).
# A copy of what the lock holds.
#
# What comes back is **stamped**: it carries where it came from, so a `set`
# that means *only if nothing changed since* can say which reading it means.
[fn."Locked::get"]
pub = true
touches = ["lock read"]
sync = true
locks = true
signature = "[T](self: ref Locked[T]) -> Seen[T]"

