# --- Inferred from `src/*.nika` ----------------------------------------------

# `touches = []` is a **claim** and not a blank: it reaches nothing, so it orders
# against nothing (ADR-292 D3). It is written here because this entry is checked
# against the `.nika` source that produces it, and `touch::infer` reads that body
# and answers the same ([ADR-288](adr-067) D2) - the two must agree.
# The value of a decimal digit: `'7'` is `7`.
#
# A character that is not a digit is not a question this answers: it hands
# back whatever the subtraction comes to.
[fn."text::digit_value"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
ensures = ["result == c - 48"]
from = ["return"]
signature = "(c: char) -> i32"

# The whole number this text writes, or nothing where it writes none.
#
# An optional `-` or `+`, then decimal digits, and nothing else: no space, no
# `_`, no point. A number too large for an `i64` is nothing too, because the
# text does not write an `i64`. `text::parse_i64(s) ?? 0` is the usual way to
# read one; a program that has to say *why* a text is not a number looks at
# it with a grammar, where `dec[i64](digit+)` reports where it went wrong.
[fn."text::parse_i64"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
views = ["text: borrowed"]
signature = "(text: ref String) -> i64?"

# What the program was started with, and the one type here that says it **may
# cross a thread** (`crosses`, ADR-005 §1 Group B). Its parts are Rust, so
# `contracts::send` cannot walk them and would answer "nobody said" - which is
# not permission, and would have cost `cli::args` the overlapping that the
# `touches` line above bought it (ADR-292). The claim is a promise about a
# Rust type, written here and reviewed like code, exactly as `sync = true` is.
# **Text a C library owns**
# ([ADR-302](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-302.md) D8). An opaque handle with
# **no cleanup**, which is what tells it from that record's D3: a `FILE` is ours
# to close and a C string is not ours at all, so nothing here frees anything.
#
# No `crosses` line, and its absence is the honest answer rather than nobody
# saying: what the address points at is the library's, and whether that library
# is happy to have it read from another thread is not a question this file can
# answer for it.
# Text a C library owns: an address, and a zero byte somewhere after it.
#
# It is what a declaration writes where C hands back a `char *`. Nothing here
# frees it - the memory is the library's, not the program's - and `to_string`
# is how a program gets text of its own out of it. Where the library may find
# nothing, the declaration says `-> CStr?` and the program writes `??`.
[type."foreign::CStr"]
pub = true

# The one function D4 asks for, and the reason it exists: the walk to the zero
# byte is the thing a program must not have to write, so it is written once,
# inside `unsafe`, here.
#
# `throws` in the one way this can fail - bytes that are not UTF-8 - which is
# what `fs::read_to_string` fails on for the same reason. *There is no text* was
# the second until [ADR-302](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-302.md) D13 made it
# a **value**: a declaration says `-> CStr?` where the library may find nothing,
# and `getenv` finding nothing is an ordinary Tuesday rather than an error.
# A copy of the text, owned by whoever asked for it.
#
# It fails in **one** way: the bytes may not be UTF-8, which text in this
# language is - the same failure `fs::read_to_string` has. *There is no text*
# is a value rather than a failure, because a declaration says `-> CStr?` where
# the library may find nothing. The `unsafe` walk to the zero byte is here so
# that no program has to write one.
[fn."foreign::CStr::to_string"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
# The doc below already said it: *the same failure `fs::read_to_string` has*.
# Since ADR-280 D5 that failure has a name, so this says it in the column too.
throws = ["io::IoError"]
signature = "(self) -> String"

# The arguments the program was started with.
#
# It may cross to another thread, so a task may be handed one.
[type."cli::Args"]
pub = true
crosses = true

# **Where a path may go** ([ADR-108](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-108.md) D2),
# and the one type in this file whose whole job is to be a **required argument**.
# `Dir(store)` resolves the name under that directory and refuses one that would
# leave it; `Anywhere` performs no check and says so in the word a review looks
# for, which `nikaia --trust` lists per site (D4).
#
# **Two variants and no shorthand.** Not a bare `String` where a `Root` is wanted,
# which would be a conversion rule this language does not have, and not a second
# spelling beside the variant.
# Where a path may go.
#
# `fs::Root::Dir(store)` resolves the name under that directory and throws
# `io::IoError::Outside` where it would leave it — resolved and compared
# component by component, so `/data` is not a prefix of `/data2` and a symlink out
# is the same answer as a `..` out. `fs::Root::Anywhere` performs no check: it does
# not say *checked*, it says *I answer for this*, and `nikaia --explain --trust`
# lists every site that writes it.
#
# Every path-taking entry takes one, right after the path, with no default and no
# exception for a literal — a relative name is resolved against the working
# directory, and whoever controls that directory has changed what the name means
# without changing a line of the program.
[type."fs::Root"]
pub = true
crosses = true
compares = true
# **The cases, and what `Dir` carries.** Without them the checker knew the
# variant names only by the path a program wrote, and `fs::Root::Dir("site")`
# reached `rustc` as a `&str` where `fs.rs` declares `Dir(String)`: the literal
# is made a `String` of its own only where the checker knows the payload is one
# (ADR-282 D4), and for a `std` enum it knows that from this column alone.
variants = ["Dir(fs::Path)", "Anywhere"]

# A file held as memory: the pages arrive as they are touched rather than being
