# **Reading it is a page fault** (ADR-281 D34): the pages arrive as they are
# touched, so `mapped[i]` is a disk read with no call in the source to hang a
# `touches` on. It neither suspends nor takes a lock, which is why neither
# `NK2202` nor `NK2203` has anything to say about it and `NK2201` does.
touches = ["file read"]

# A step of this one can **fail**, and that is a fact a caller cannot get from
# anywhere else: a `for` over it is a place the enclosing function can fail
# from, and the compiler makes that function say `throws` (ADR-025 D1, `NK2701`).
# What a `std` call fails with.
#
# Part I 7.1's error type, for the library: `NotFound(what)` where the thing is
# not there, `PermissionDenied(what)` where this program may not have it,
# `NotText(what)` where the bytes are not UTF-8, `Outside(what)` where a name
# would leave the `fs::Root` it was given, and `Other(what)` for
# everything else the operating system said. A `catch` that only passes the
# failure on names nothing; one that takes it apart writes `use std::io` and
# `io::IoError::NotFound(p)`.
[type."io::IoError"]
pub = true
crosses = true
# **`compares = true`** ([ADR-204](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-204.md) D1),
# and like `crosses` it is a claim reviewed like code: this type's parts are Rust,
# so nothing in the compiler can walk them. An easy claim here — every variant
# holds a `String` or an `fs::Path`, and a name compares by its bytes.
#
# **Absence is not permission**: a `std` type without this line does not compare,
# and `NK1188` says so on the author's line rather than letting `rustc` say it
# about a generated file.
compares = true
# **The cases, so a handler's `match` can be total** (Part I 3.4). Without them
# `NK1151` asked for an `else` on a `match` that had covered every variant — a
# correct program refused, with a way out that makes *this type gaining a
# variant* silent forever after, which is the one thing
# [ADR-291](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-291.md) D19 exists to prevent.
#
# Each carries what it was about, which is
# [ADR-280](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-280.md) D5's *the variant is the
# kind and it carries the thing*.
variants = ["NotFound(fs::Path)", "PermissionDenied(fs::Path)", "NotText(String)", "Outside(fs::Path)", "Other(String)"]

# **What a parse fails with** ([ADR-296](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-296.md)
# D1), and the end of the last `"?"` in this tree. A `dsl` entry rule's ledger
# entry wrote `throws = ["?"]` because a parse fails with a rendered string and
# a string is not a type; seven of the corpus' eight `main`s carried that into
# their own set, and each of them has a **sum of two named members** now.
#
# No module in front, which is `Overtaken`'s shape: a program never writes a
# path to it, because it arrives in a `catch`.
#
# It carries the message and nothing else (D2). What the backend renders is a
# whole diagnostic - the headline, the line with a caret, what else was possible
# - and it is what a program already printed. Fields a program could *read* are
# a second question and nobody has asked it.
# What a parse fails with.
#
# A `dsl` entry rule hands this back where its input is not the shape the
# grammar says. It prints the whole diagnostic the backend produced: the
# headline, the line with a caret under what was found, and what else was
# possible there. A `catch` that only passes it on names nothing; one that tells
# it apart from an `io::IoError` writes `match error { … }`.
[type."ParseError"]
pub = true
crosses = true

# Standard input, one line at a time.
#
# A **step** of it can fail, because a step reads the pipe - so a `for` over it
# is a place the function around it can fail from, and that function says
# `throws`.
[type."io::Lines"]
pub = true
iterates = "throws"

# The type several parts of a program own at once (Part I 6.2), and the one type
# here whose `deref` entry above a caller is not meant to notice: a function that
# takes `ref Connection` accepts a `ref Shared[Connection]`, which is ADR-042 D2's
# rescue rather than a rule of this file's own.
#
# No `crosses` line, deliberately, and its absence is not "nobody said" here.
# `contracts::send` keeps `Shared` in its **container** list since ADR-037 D6, so
# a `Shared[T]` is answered by what `T` is: `Shared[String]` crosses and
# `Shared[Locked[i32]]` is undecided, for the lock's sake and not the count's. A
# `crosses = true` written here would answer for both, which is the one thing
# that is not true of the type.
# **What a `spawn` hands back** (Part I 8.2,
# [ADR-055](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-055.md) D5).
#
# No `crosses`, and here its absence really is "nobody said": whether a handle
# may cross a thread is a question about the value the task produces and about
# the executor that owns it, and the multi-threaded executor is not built
# (ADR-055 §6 step 1's `yes` half). An undecided crossing is handed on rather
# than read as permission (ADR-010 D1), which is what this file's own note
# above says.
# What a `spawn` hands back: the one end of a task that is still running.
#
# `join` is where the two meet again.
[type."TaskHandle"]
pub = true

# `.join()` is where two tasks meet again, and it is a **suspension point**: the
# task fills a slot and wakes whoever is waiting on it, so joining gives the
# thread up rather than holding it. Hence no `sync` line, which is this file's
# way of saying it can pause - and the emitter turns that into the `.await`
# (ADR-055 D2).
#
# `T` is bound by the receiver (ADR-288), so one line answers for every
# `TaskHandle` a program writes. It takes `self` by value because a value can
# only be joined once.
#
# No `throws`: a task's failure is the task's, and what `join` hands back is
# whatever the body's last statement was. A task whose body can fail hands back
# a value that says so, which is the same shape any other function has.
# Wait for the task and take what it produced.
#
# It gives the thread up rather than holding it, so other work runs while this
# waits. A handle can be joined once, because joining takes it.
[fn."TaskHandle::join"]
pub = true
signature = "[T](self: TaskHandle[T]) -> T"

# **One mechanism, said three ways rather than three things**
# ([ADR-292](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-292.md) D14): a program's own
# cancel, a `select`'s loser and ADR-284's C `cancel` do the same thing to a
# task - it stops at its current pause point, its values are torn down, and a
# `cleanup` that pauses is adopted by the runtime.
#
# `sync = true`, and that is not a slip: this is a *request*. It sets a flag and
# wakes the task, and the caller does not wait for the teardown because it
# should not pay for it (D2).
#
# No `touches`, which is the fail-closed answer: what a cancel orders against is
# whatever the task was doing, and that is not a set this file can name.
#
# It takes the handle, as `join` does, which is what makes §4's open question -
# *can a cancelled task be observed to have been cancelled?* - one no program
# can ask.
# Stop the task.
#
# It stops at its current pause point and its values are torn down; a `cleanup`
# that pauses is finished in the background. This does not wait for any of
# that. Cancelling takes the handle, so a cancelled task is never joined.
[fn."TaskHandle::cancel"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "[T](self: TaskHandle[T])"

# A value several parts of a program own at once.
#
# A caller is not meant to notice it: a function taking `ref Connection` accepts a
# `ref Shared[Connection]`. Handing a handle on by value makes a second one rather
# than taking the first away.
[type."Shared"]
pub = true

# --- A span of time (ADR-150) ------------------------------------------------
#
# **The language learns nothing here** (D4): no keyword, no suffix literal, no
# type in the grammar. What it costs is this block - one type, ten methods and
# one call - and the reason it is ten rather than five is ADR-285's two integer
# types: `5` is an `i32` where nothing asks otherwise and `count.minutes()` on a
# length is an `i64`, so a file holding both needs both halves or one of the two
# is refused in the language below's words (Part III C.1).
#
# `crosses = true` is a claim reviewed like code, and it is an easy one: a
# duration is a number of ticks and nothing else, so it may go anywhere.
# `copies = true` for the same reason (ADR-150 D1, *a value that copies*):
# without it a span handed to `time::sleep` was gone for the line after.
# A span of time: how long something takes, how long to wait, how long is too
# long.
#
# It is written `5.seconds()` rather than `5s` - a literal has no suffix in
# this language, and a rule with an exception in it is one a reader has to
# remember. `seconds`, `millis`, `micros`, `minutes` and `hours` are the five
# names, on either integer type.
#
# **A span computed while the program is built is written as its constructor
# over its parts** (ADR-318 D5): a `comptime` or an option's default of this
# type crosses as `time::Duration::new(30, 0)`.
[type."time::Duration"]
pub = true
crosses = true
compares = true
copies = true
constant = "time::Duration::new(secs: u64, nanos: u32)"

# The constructor `constant` names, for the compiler and not for a program: a
# span is made with `seconds`, `millis` and the rest.
[fn."time::Duration::new"]
pub = false
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(secs: u64, nanos: u32) -> time::Duration"

# `touches = []` and `sync = true` for the arithmetic entries' reason: a pure
# computation that reaches no resource. A count below zero is no time at all
# rather than an abort, which is the whole of what the implementation decides.
# This many hours, as a span of time.
[fn."i32::hours"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> time::Duration"

# This many minutes, as a span of time.
[fn."i32::minutes"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> time::Duration"

# This many seconds, as a span of time.
[fn."i32::seconds"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> time::Duration"

# This many thousandths of a second, as a span of time.
[fn."i32::millis"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> time::Duration"

# This many millionths of a second, as a span of time.
[fn."i32::micros"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> time::Duration"

# This many hours, as a span of time.
[fn."i64::hours"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> time::Duration"

# This many minutes, as a span of time.
[fn."i64::minutes"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> time::Duration"

# This many seconds, as a span of time.
[fn."i64::seconds"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> time::Duration"

# This many thousandths of a second, as a span of time.
[fn."i64::millis"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> time::Duration"

# This many millionths of a second, as a span of time.
[fn."i64::micros"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> time::Duration"

# **Read back** ([ADR-150](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-150.md) D5): the
# five units a span is made in, as a count. Whole units, cut off - 90 seconds
# is one minute - and an `i64`, a count's type (ADR-285 D1). A span longer than
# an `i64` holds reads as `i64::MAX`, the constructors' saturation mirrored.
# `touches = []` and `sync = true` for the arithmetic entries' reason. Rust's
# own `as_secs` and kin are not here: one spelling per unit.
# How many whole hours the span holds, cut off.
[fn."time::Duration::in_hours"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> i64"

# How many whole minutes the span holds, cut off.
[fn."time::Duration::in_minutes"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> i64"

# How many whole seconds the span holds, cut off.
[fn."time::Duration::in_seconds"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> i64"

# How many whole thousandths of a second the span holds, cut off.
[fn."time::Duration::in_millis"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> i64"

# How many whole millionths of a second the span holds, cut off.
[fn."time::Duration::in_micros"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self) -> i64"

# **In `std::time` and not in the prelude**
# ([ADR-313](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-313.md) D2): the rule the list is
# built from is that nothing in it pauses, and this is the one entry here that
# does. So it is `use std::time` and `time::sleep(…)`, and Part II 12.4's line
# is written that way.
#
# **A suspension point**, which is what the missing `sync` line says: the thread
# is given up rather than held, so a program that sleeps runs its other tasks
# while it does. That is the whole difference between this and the language
# below's `thread::sleep`, and it is why the entry is here rather than nowhere.
#
# No `touches`, and its absence is the fail-closed answer rather than an
# omission: what a wait orders against is everything, because the point of it is
# that time passes in between.
# Wait out a span of time.
#
# The thread is given up rather than held, so other tasks run while this waits.
[fn."time::sleep"]
pub = true
signature = "(span: time::Duration)"

# --- Message passing (ADR-149) -----------------------------------------------
#
# **No language construct** (D1): two values, two methods, and the tuple `let`
# that binds them was already built (ADR-291). What this block costs is four
# entries.
#
# `-> (Sender[T], Receiver[T])` names the shape a `let` destructures. The
# variable stays unbound - nothing at the call says what the channel carries -
# so it becomes `?`, which is ADR-288's rule and is enough: what the two methods
# need from it is the *type name*, so that `tx.send(…)` resolves to the entry
# below and takes the `.await` its missing `sync` line asks for.
# A channel of `capacity` values, and its two ends.
#
# There is no unbounded channel: a capacity is a promise about memory, and an
# unbounded one is a `send` that never pauses. A program that wants *unbounded*
# writes a large number - and has said it.
[fn."channel::bounded"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "[T](capacity: i64) -> (channel::Sender[T], channel::Receiver[T])"

# The sending end of a channel.
#
# Handing it into a `spawn` is where the crossing check asks whether what the
# channel carries may go to another thread.
[type."channel::Sender"]
pub = true

# The receiving end of a channel.
#
# `recv` hands back a `T?`, and `null` means every sender is gone.
[type."channel::Receiver"]
pub = true

# **A suspension point**, which is the missing `sync` line (D2): a `send` waits
# for room where the channel is full, so a `sync` body cannot send on one and
# the compiler names the promise in the way - without a rule about channels,
# because the column *is* the rule. That is what a bounded channel is for:
# back-pressure is a pause, and a pause is something this ledger already talks
# about.
#
# No `touches`, which is the fail-closed answer: what a send orders against is
# whatever the other end does with the value.
# Put a value in, waiting for room where there is none.
#
# It gives the thread up rather than holding it, so other work runs while this
# waits.
[fn."channel::Sender::send"]
pub = true
signature = "[T](self: ref channel::Sender[T], value: ?)"

# **`-> T?`**, and the `?` is D3: a closed channel is the ordinary **end** of a
# stream rather than a failure, so `??` is what reads it (Part I 3.5) and
# nothing here `throws`.
# The next value, or `null` where every sender is gone.
#
# A closed channel is the ordinary end of a stream and not a failure, so `??`
# is what reads it. It gives the thread up rather than holding it.
[fn."channel::Receiver::recv"]
pub = true
signature = "[T](self: ref channel::Receiver[T]) -> T?"

# --- The collections outside the prelude (ADR-313 D3) ------------------------
#
# **`HashMap` is the first thing outside the list**, and it is the name that
# makes that a rule rather than a tidy-up: a map is common enough to argue for,
# and `use std::collections` is one line, and a list that grows by *common
# enough* has no floor.
#
# They are here rather than in the compiler's built-in names because that is what
# puts them in a **module**: an entry keyed `collections::HashMap` is one a
# program reaches through its prefix, which is the whole of the rule.
# A table from keys to values.
#
# It is reached through its module: `use std::collections` and
# `collections::HashMap` where it is needed (Part I, 1.3).
[type."collections::HashMap"]
pub = true

# A set of values, in no order.
#
# It is reached through its module, as every name outside the prelude is.
[type."collections::HashSet"]
pub = true

# A table from keys to values, kept in the keys' order.
#
# It is reached through its module, as every name outside the prelude is.
[type."collections::BTreeMap"]
pub = true

# A set of values, kept in their order.
#
# It is reached through its module, as every name outside the prelude is.
[type."collections::BTreeSet"]
pub = true

# --- `process`: another program, started and waited for (ADR-243) ----------
#
# [ADR-290](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-290.md) D14 put a subprocess in
# `std` on the socket's own argument: an operating-system resource, not a
# protocol. **No `touches`**, which is the claim that it touches everything: a
# child may write any file this program reads, so it keeps its place against
# every other effect (ADR-292 D3's reading of an absent column).
#
# **What it hands back is `untrusted`**: the program that ran may have printed
# what somebody else sent it, and a map keyed on its output is hashed with the
# keyed function (ADR-010 D2).

# What a program that ran to its end left behind.
#
# `code` is its exit code, or `-1` where it was ended by a signal and has none;
# `ok` is whether that code is `0`; `stdout` and `stderr` are everything it wrote
# to each, as text.
[type."process::Output"]
pub = true
crosses = true
fields = ["pub code: i64", "pub ok: bool", "pub stdout: String", "pub stderr: String"]

# **`args: Vec[String]`, handed over**, and not `?`: an empty list has no
# element type of its own, and a parameter that names none left `run("pwd", [])`
# for `rustc` to guess at (ADR-135 D2 - the use answers it). A list of literals
# is built into text of its own where it is kept (ADR-282 D4), so the ordinary
# call is written as it reads.
# Start a program, wait for it to finish, and hand back what it said.
#
# `program` is looked for on `PATH` where it has no `/` in it; `dir` is the
# directory it starts in. A non-zero exit is not a failure - it is `code` and
# `ok`, and the caller decides what it means. It fails where the program cannot
# be started, and where what it wrote is not UTF-8.
#
# The wait gives the thread up, so other tasks run while the program does.
[fn."process::run"]
pub = true
throws = ["io::IoError"]
provenance = "untrusted"
keeps = ["args"]
signature = "(program: ref String, args: Vec[String]; dir: ref String = \".\") -> process::Output"

# --- `net`: the socket `std` lends (ADR-289 D6) ------------------------------
#
# [ADR-289](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-289.md) D2's subtraction carried one
# item further: what was pushed out of `std` is the *protocol and the framework*,
# and a socket is neither - it is an operating-system resource of exactly the kind
# `fs` and `io` already own. The `http` package is Nikaia and cannot syscall, so
# the line falls here.
#
# **`touches` says `socket` and never which socket**, for the reason `lock` says
# `lock`: a listener could be named by its address and a *connection* is what a
# program actually touches, and telling two of those apart is the alias analysis
# ADR-281 D30 refuses to make a program's compilation depend on. Two reads do not
# conflict - reads never do - and a write orders against every socket touch.
#
# **Nothing here is `sync`**, and that is the point: `accept`, `read` and `write`
# give the thread up rather than holding it, on ADR-121 D4's awaitable readiness.
# The same park `fs` uses, so one thread serves many connections at
# `user_parallelism = no` exactly as at `yes`.

# A bound socket, waiting for connections.
#
# `net::listen("127.0.0.1:8080")` makes one. Which address a *server* binds when
# nobody says is the server's own question and not this one's.
[type."net::Listener"]
pub = true
# A listener may be handed to a task: it is a file descriptor and an address,
# and nothing in it is tied to the thread it was made on.
crosses = true

# One connection, open until it is dropped.
#
# Reading hands back what the peer sent, and **empty means the peer closed** -
# which is the one thing a caller has to know to write a loop. `close` is for a
# program that wants the close at a named moment.
[type."net::Connection"]
pub = true
crosses = true

# Bind an address and start listening.
#
# It fails where the address cannot be bound - which is usually somebody else
# already listening there.
[fn."net::listen"]
pub = true
touches = ["socket write"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
signature = "(address: ref String) -> net::Listener"

# Connect to an address.
#
# It fails where nothing is listening there.
[fn."net::connect"]
pub = true
touches = ["socket write"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
signature = "(address: ref String) -> net::Connection"

# The next connection, waiting for one where there is none yet.
#
# It gives the thread up while it waits, so one thread serves many connections.
[fn."net::Listener::accept"]
pub = true
touches = ["socket read"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
signature = "(self: ref Listener) -> net::Connection"

# The address it is **bound** to, which is what a caller that asked for port `0`
# needs to know. Memory, so it touches nothing and cannot pause.
# The address this listener is bound to.
#
# A program that asked for port `0` finds out here which port it got.
[fn."net::Listener::address"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self: ref Listener) -> String"

# **The first untrusted source `std` has** (ADR-010 D2), which this file's own
# header predicted by name: *the untrusted ones - `http`, `net`, `db` - arrive
# with the modules that have them*. Somebody else chose these bytes.
# The next bytes the peer sent, waiting for them where there are none yet.
#
# **Empty means the peer closed.** What comes back is **untrusted**: somebody
# else chose these bytes.
[fn."net::Connection::read"]
pub = true
touches = ["socket read"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
provenance = "untrusted"
signature = "(mut self: ref Connection) -> Bytes"

# `?` for the bytes, for `fs::write`'s reason: it takes a `Bytes`, a `ref String`\
# and a buffer, and writing one of them here would refuse a correct program.
# All of the bytes, waiting for room where there is none yet.
#
# All of them, because a partial write is not something a caller can do anything
# useful with: the loop that would follow one is inside this.
[fn."net::Connection::write"]
pub = true
touches = ["socket write"]
throws = ["io::IoError"]
signature = "(mut self: ref Connection, bytes: ?)"

# Who is on the other end.
[fn."net::Connection::peer"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self: ref Connection) -> String"

# Close it now rather than when it is dropped.
#
# Dropping one closes it, which is the language below's answer; a program that
# wants the close at a named moment writes this.
[fn."net::Connection::close"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["socket write"]
signature = "(self: Connection)"

# --- `http1`: HTTP/1.1's text half (ADR-289 D13) -------------------------------
#
# **Not named `http`**, and that is not a taste: a program reaches a *package* by
# that word (ADR-289 D1), and a `std` module of the same name would make
# `http::Response` mean two things. This file's own note about a crate's word
# colliding with a module's says what that day costs; this is the day not to
# create one.
#
# **The parser is Rust and moving it into a Nikaia grammar is a later step**
# (D5): self-hosting the protocol is not the MVP, and the route it will take is
# ADR-290 D17's.
#
# `sync = true` on all of it: this is text handling over bytes that are already
# there. It cannot pause and it reaches no resource, which is what lets a server
# call it between two things that do.
#
# **Where the line between this and a package runs.** This module holds
# **framing** - where a head ends, what its lines say, how many bytes of body the
# head promised - and a package holds **meaning**: what a path is for, what a
# handler is, what goes back. `Buffer` is on this side of the line because
# deciding that a head has *not arrived yet* is reading HTTP/1.1, not reading a
# request.
#
# **What it hands back is not marked `untrusted`**, and the reason is that it
# does not need to be: what came off the socket already is (`net::Connection::read`,
# ADR-010 D2), and provenance travels with the bytes rather than being re-stated
# by everything that reads them.

# The bytes of one request as they arrive, and the questions HTTP/1.1 can ask of
# them.
#
# It is written `http1::Buffer()`. A caller reads a chunk from a socket, `take`s
# it, and asks `head_end` again.
[type."http1::Buffer"]
pub = true
crosses = true

# What the head of one HTTP/1.1 request says.
#
# The two methods this speaks are `GET` and `POST`; anything else is a refusal
# and not a guess. A path is **not** decoded — what a `%20` means is the
# program's question.
[type."http1::Head"]
pub = true
crosses = true

# Nothing read yet. It is written `http1::Buffer()`.
[fn."http1::Buffer::new"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "() -> http1::Buffer"

# `?` for the bytes, `net::Connection::write`'s reason: what a caller has is a
# `Bytes`, and a `Bytes` here would refuse the `ref String` a test hands it.
# Add what the socket just handed over.
[fn."http1::Buffer::take"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(mut self: ref Buffer, more: ?)"

# How many bytes have arrived.
[fn."http1::Buffer::len"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self: ref Buffer) -> i64"

# **`0` is *not yet*** rather than a missing value, and the shape was chosen for
# what it lets a caller write: `while ended == 0` is one condition where a loop
# over an absence is a condition plus the unwrapping after it. A head cannot end
# at byte zero - the shortest one there is is `GET / HTTP/1.0\r\n\r\n` - so the
# two readings cannot collide.
# Where the head ends, or **`0` for *not yet***: the blank line that ends a head
# has not arrived, so read more and ask again.
#
# A head over `cap` fails rather than growing - the bytes are somebody else's -
# and it fails as soon as the buffer is over it, because a head that has not
# ended and is already too long never will.
[fn."http1::Buffer::head_end"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
throws = ["io::IoError"]
signature = "(self: ref Buffer, cap: i64) -> i64"

# What the head says, where `head_end` said there is one.
#
# Every failure here is what a server sends back: a request line that is not
# three words, a version this does not speak, a method it does not answer, a
# `content-length` that is not a count, a `transfer-encoding` at all.
[fn."http1::Buffer::head"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
throws = ["io::IoError"]
signature = "(self: ref Buffer) -> http1::Head"

# The bytes from `at` on, as text.
#
# **Owned**, and the copy is not an oversight: this is the body, and a body goes
# into a request a handler is given, which owns it.
[fn."http1::Buffer::text_from"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
throws = ["io::IoError"]
signature = "(self: ref Buffer, at: i64) -> String"

# `GET` or `POST`, as it was written.
[fn."http1::Head::method"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self: ref Head) -> ref(self) String"

# The path, **without** the query string, and **not decoded** (ADR-289 D20, which
# writes `path()` and `query()` as two things).
[fn."http1::Head::path"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self: ref Head) -> ref(self) String"

# The whole request target as it was written, query string and all.
#
# What a log wants, and the one place the bytes the client chose are handed over
# untouched.
[fn."http1::Head::target"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self: ref Head) -> ref(self) String"

# **`String?` and not an empty string** (ADR-289 D20: *Kap 3.5's nullable*): a name
# the client did not write and one it wrote empty are two different facts, and a
# server that read them as one would answer a question nobody asked.
# What the query string says under this name, or nothing where it says nothing.
#
# **Nothing is decoded**: a `%20` stays `%20` and a `+` stays a `+`, because which
# of the two a `+` means depends on who wrote the form. **A name with no `=` has
# an empty value**, which is not the same as being absent: `?debug` says the name
# was written.
[fn."http1::Head::query"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self: ref Head, name: ?) -> ref(self) String?"

# What the client sent under this name, or nothing where it sent none.
#
# **Case-insensitive, as the protocol is** (ADR-289 D20). Where a client sent the
# same name twice this is the **first**: joining them with a comma is what the
# protocol says a *list-valued* header means, and which headers those are is not
# something this module knows.
[fn."http1::Head::header"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self: ref Head, name: ?) -> ref(self) String?"

# How many headers there are, which is what a program counts before it decides a
# client is being unreasonable.
[fn."http1::Head::headers"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self: ref Head) -> i64"

# What `Content-Length` said, or `0` where it said nothing.
[fn."http1::Head::length"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self: ref Head) -> i64"

# How many bytes of the buffer the head took, so a caller knows where the body
# starts.
[fn."http1::Head::size"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self: ref Head) -> i64"

# Whether the client asked for the connection to stay open.
#
# **Read and not honoured**: `Connection: close` is the MVP's scope, and a server
# that read the header and ignored it silently would be lying to a client that
# asked.
[fn."http1::Head::keep_alive"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(self: ref Head) -> bool"

# --- `Bytes`: how many there are ----------------------------------------------
#
# The one thing a program has to be able to ask a `Bytes`, because
# `net::Connection::read` says **empty means the peer closed** and a loop over a
# socket cannot be written without the question.
# How many bytes there are.
#
# After `net::Connection::read`, **zero means the peer closed**.
[fn."Bytes::len"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(ref self) -> i64"

# Whether there are none.
[fn."Bytes::is_empty"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(ref self) -> bool"
